Title: LMCache: save finished conversations and continue new prompts from them (llama-server)
Add-on: replaces 0005 (no bundled zstd, nothing in libllama, no llama.h change). Off by default.
  --lmcache                  keep the state of each finished conversation (KV cache + recurrent state + up to N checkpoints) and continue a
                             new prompt from the saved one that saves the most work, from any slot
  --lmcache-chunk-size N     prompts are matched in steps of N tokens (default 256)
  --lmcache-ram-mb N         RAM for the saved conversations (default 8192)
  --lmcache-checkpoints N    checkpoints kept per saved conversation, half the earliest and half the latest (default 4)
  --lmcache-disk             also keep them on disk; they survive restarts
  --lmcache-disk-dir DIR     (default ./data/lmcache)
  --lmcache-disk-max-mb N    disk space, oldest files go first (default 65536)
  --lmcache-disk-idle-sec N  write to disk only after N seconds without requests, and at shutdown (default 30)
  --lmcache-blend, -blend-recompute, -blend-min-tokens, -compress, --(no-)lmcache-async-save: accepted and ignored (old settings keep working)
Restoring puts the slot exactly where it would be had it processed the saved conversation itself (state, tokens, checkpoints); the server's
own prompt-reuse logic then keeps the shared tokens and, for recurrent models like Qwen3.6, goes back to a checkpoint before the first
difference. So a saved conversation is used when it is a whole prefix of the prompt and when the prompt changes inside it (compacted or
edited conversations), whenever that resumes further than the slot's own state. At most 2 saved conversations per conversation (the latest
and the previous turn). Disk writes happen on a background thread after the server has been quiet, and earlier turns are not written.
Fixes against the old 0005: it restored a longer state than n_past and skipped the server's "reprocess" safety (wrong text on Qwen3.6); it
saved twice per request; it kept checkpoints of other tokens. Not used with a draft model / MTP or with images.

